				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nornoa" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7022260); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7022260&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606437833" alt="Nornoa" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7022260)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nornoa" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7022260); return false;" >Nornoa</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606435299 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Nov 2020 00:01</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4828922">
									

<p>Perhaps I should walk through one example really quick. Forgive any typos, I am on mobile atm.</p>
<p>SCP XXXX undergoes some testing with scp 343 and comes through ok. Upon the records being filed with exclusion site, the exclusion site gets a version of this report, let's call it Report 1A. However, later that month a containment breach happens, but SCP XXXX (not uncontained, just in a site during a breach) survives, getting logged in a incident report which we will call 2B.</p>
<p>Upon upload to the exlusion site, they get 3 reports. One copy of report 2B, where scp xxxx survived the breach. The other two reports that appear are Report 1B, where differences might be as minor as different things spoken, and 2A, in which 682 eats scp xxxx during the breach. Upon an interview with researchers present at the first test, they remember events as detailed in report 1B.</p>
<p>This should answer the question of what's going on, as clearly in one reality, he had died in the breach while in the one they currently reside in he lived. Exclusion sites are immune to time shifts, especially at this scale, so they are able to somewhat track his lives through the discrepencies in the report. The only question is if he is creating these realities with every death, or merely exploring them through his choices. The former is a serious threat, while the latter means he is a traveler with the logs acting like pages from a journal left behind.</p>
<p>Containment is as cold as the foundation, using what effectively amounts to brainwashing to ensure that he keeps living life in the same way that led him to the Foundation. Now, they can only really detect this anomaly through the exclusion sites reports (at least at first), so my way to intro him will likely be as a D-class, who just so happens get included in a report that results in a duplicate report being discovered by the exclusion site during routine backups.</p>
<p>As for &quot;what makes this time loop stand out?&quot; There's a few things. Firstly, this is written from an outsider's point of view, being the exlcusion site reports. Secondly, this guy has no exit, unlike most other time loops where eventally they get that escape from infinite repetitions. Thirdly, this guy isn't smart. Bill Murray was mastering all kinds of skills from piano to ice sculpting, but this guys struggles to remember himself. The human brain has limited capacity, and this dude is pretty average at best outside of the anomoly.</p>
<p>The 4th thing as you mentioned is scale. Due to starting as a baby everytime, by the time he becomes a d-class he has made way too many chioces for him to remember perfectly, let alone repeat. This allows the butterfly effect to really have some power compared to day long time loops where you aren't able to change most routines or circumstances.</p>
<p>I hope that answered your questions. This SCP isn't generally going to be as flashy or otherworldly. It's the story of a man living way too long in what amounts to basic human containment in foundation custody. He's not necessarily a direct threat, but his existance poses many problems that the Foundation would prefer to minimize if possible.</p>

							</div>

								
					
